# Fuzukue changelog

Release changes and known limitations for users installing or upgrading Fuzukue.
Version numbers follow Semantic Versioning.

## 0.2.4 - Unreleased

### Added

- Reorder reading-pane tabs by dragging their labels left or right with the mouse. Show the insertion position and automatically scroll overflowing tabs when holding the pointer at either edge.
- Preserve the selected document, per-tab zoom and reading position when reordering. Keyboard tab switching and the next tab selected on close follow the new order.
- Cancel tab drags on Escape, pointer cancellation or window focus loss, and disable reordering during file and PDF operations.

### Known limitations

- This version has not been published. Native Windows/WebView2 mouse interaction still requires interactive validation.
- Tabs and their order are not restored after restarting.

## 0.2.3 - Unreleased

### Fixed

- Rasterize local SVG figures to approximately 300 dpi PNG during PDF export, retaining their layout and restoring the original SVG after success, cancellation or failure.
- Preserve validated PNG images embedded in local SVG, including Matplotlib colorbars, while continuing to reject external and non-PNG image references.

### Added

- Place consecutive page numbers at the bottom center of every PDF page.

### Known limitations

- This version has not been published. Native WebView2 PDF capture still requires interactive validation.

## [0.2.2]

### Fixed

- Restore the v0.2.0 multi-document tabs and per-tab zoom that were missing from the v0.2.1 development base.
- Reload only the selected tab without creating duplicate tabs or changing other documents. Preserve tab order, zoom and scroll positions, and keep normal duplicate-file opens focused on their existing cached tab.
- Preserve the current preview on read failures and allow retry. If refreshed content cannot be rendered, retain the previous preview but block stale PDF export until a successful reload.

### Included functionality

- Open up to 20 Markdown documents using multi-select or drag-and-drop, switch with Ctrl+Tab / Ctrl+Shift+Tab, and close with Ctrl+W.
- Retain each tab's rendered content, outline and reading position; zoom documents independently from 50% to 200% while PDF output remains at 100%.

### Known limitations

- Interactive Windows/WebView2 rendering, native dialogs, drag-and-drop and PDF fidelity still require manual validation.
- Tabs are not restored after restarting, and tab reordering is not supported.

## [0.2.1] - Development 2026-10-28 (not published)

### Added

- Reload the current local Markdown file from disk with a toolbar button, without opening a file dialog. The preview, outline, math, diagrams and local images are rebuilt from the latest contents.
- Disable reload when no local file is open or an operation is in progress. Read failures show an error and keep the current preview available for retry.

### Known limitations

- Native Windows/WebView2 rendering and PDF fidelity still require interactive validation.
- PDF files cannot be opened; reload applies to the Markdown source document.

## [0.1.0] - 2026-10-26

### Breaking changes

None. This is the first release; no migration is required.

### Added

- Open a single local UTF-8 Markdown document through a native dialog or drag-and-drop in a Rust/Tauri 2 Windows x64 application.
- Read GitHub-style tables, task lists and fenced code, plus Qiita-style notes, code filenames, footnotes and details.
- Render TeX with KaTeX using dollar delimiters, bracket delimiters including `\(\bar r\)`, and fenced math blocks.
- Render Mermaid diagrams offline and display relative PNG/SVG images or remote HTTP(S) images.
- Navigate headings, switch light/dark themes and use the Japanese reading interface.
- Save an A4 PDF directly to a selected destination through WebView2, without a print dialog.
- Confine local image access to the document directory and show rendering errors before allowing PDF export.
- Provide an unsigned Windows x64 NSIS installer and standalone executable, English/Japanese documentation and an MIT project license.

### Fixed

- Preserve safe SVG colors, lines, fonts and local glyph references instead of turning Matplotlib SVG figures into black rectangles.
- Avoid development-server port collisions by reserving an available loopback port and closing only the launcher's own server.
- Pin dependencies to versions retrievable from the npm mirror used during validation without changing organization-managed npm settings.

### Known limitations

- Black rendering outside linked images has been reported and remains unresolved; the SVG fix does not establish that this separate problem is solved.
- Interactive Windows smoke testing and native WebView2 PDF fidelity checks remain incomplete. Automated unit/browser checks and Windows-target builds do not replace those checks.
- Windows may warn about unsigned binaries. The installer needs internet access to obtain WebView2 if it is missing; the standalone executable requires WebView2 already installed.
- The application is a read-only, single-document viewer, not an exact GitHub/Qiita clone; editing, tabs, file watching, file associations and video/SNS embeds are not supported.
- Remote images contact their hosts. Arbitrary SVG CSS, animation and external SVG resources are intentionally disabled.

